- Retrieved practices:
- Avoid invalid pointer-to-member access in C++
WHAT: Using .* or ->* with a pointer-to-member that refers to a member not present in the dynamic type of the object, or using a null pointer-to-member, causes undefined behavior.
WHY: Undefined behavior can lead to memory corruption, crashes, or exploitable security vulnerabilities.
HOW: Ensure the dynamic type of the object actually defines the targeted member before using .* or ->*; never use null pointer-to-member values; avoid casting pointer-to-member values across unrelated types.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `void (Base::*pm)() = nullptr; (obj.*pm)();` After (safe): `void (Base::*pm)() = &Base::f; if (pm) (obj.*pm)();`

- Do not use pointer-to-member operators with null or uninitialized member pointers
WHAT: Using the pointer-to-member operator (->* or .* ) with a null or uninitialized pointer-to-member invokes undefined behavior and may call an invalid function or access a nonexistent member.
WHY: Undefined behavior can lead to crashes, arbitrary code execution, or other unpredictable security vulnerabilities.
HOW: Always initialize pointer-to-member variables to valid members before use, and check that the pointer-to-member is non-null and points to an existing member before invoking it.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `static void (D::*gptr)(); void call_memptr(D *ptr){ (ptr->*gptr)(); }` After (safe): `static void (D::*gptr)() = &D::g; void call_memptr(D *ptr){ if (gptr) (ptr->*gptr)(); }`

- Avoid information leakage when passing objects across trust boundaries
WHAT: Failing to fully initialize all fields (including padding and reserved fields) of objects or structs before copying them across a trust boundary can leak uninitialized memory contents.
WHY: Attackers can read sensitive kernel or process memory, potentially exposing secrets, internal state, or enabling further exploitation.
HOW: Before returning or sending any struct/class instance to less-trusted code, explicitly initialize or zero all fields (including padding/reserved areas), or use APIs/types that guarantee zero-initialization.
EXAMPLE (C++): Before: `MyStruct s; some_fields_set(s); send_to_user(s);` After: `MyStruct s{}; some_fields_set(s); secure_memset_unused_fields(s); send_to_user(s);`

- Do not access non-value bits of an object’s representation
WHAT: Accessing or modifying bits of an object’s representation that are not part of its value representation (for example, padding bits or internal control structures like vtable pointers) can lead to undefined behavior and object corruption.
WHY: Such undefined behavior can corrupt program state, break type and memory safety, and enable attacks such as control-flow hijacking via corrupted vtables or object layouts.
HOW: Only read non-value bits when their values are not used or assumed, and only write to padding bits; never write to bits that may contain control data (for example, vtable pointers). Prefer C++ special member functions and overloaded operators over low-level C library functions when copying or moving objects.
EXAMPLE (C++): Instead of manually manipulating object bytes, use safe copying: `S s2 = s1;` or define copy/move constructors and assignment operators; if `std::memcpy` is used, ensure it only copies trivially copyable objects and does not rely on or modify non-value bits beyond padding.

- Do not alternately input and output from a file stream without an intervening positioning call
WHAT: Alternating reads and writes on the same C++ file stream (opened for update) without an intervening flush or positioning call causes undefined behavior when the file is not at end-of-file.
WHY: Undefined behavior can corrupt data, desynchronize file contents and buffers, or cause logic errors that may be exploitable in security-sensitive code paths.
HOW: After output and before subsequent input, call std::fflush() or a file-positioning function (fseek, fsetpos, rewind, or C++ equivalents via seekg/seekp); after input and before subsequent output (when not at EOF), call a file-positioning function (seekg/seekp) to reset the file buffer state.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `fstream f("data", ios::in|ios::out); f << x; f >> y;` After (safe): `fstream f("data", ios::in|ios::out); f << x; f.seekg(0, ios::cur); f >> y;`



